# SYNTAX TEST "text.html.tiddlywiki5" "A pragma stands only before the first block"

# TiddlyWiki reads pragmas in a mode of its own, which the parser leaves at the first construct no
# pragma rule matches. wikiparser.js runs parsePragmas() once, before parseBlocks(), and never
# returns to it — so a backslash directive standing after any block content parses as ordinary text.
#
# The grammar reads one anywhere. Measured against the parser over the pinned samples, this accounts
# for the bulk of the spans `npm run overreach` reports: every directive on lines the samples place
# after their opening block, and everything the grammar sub-tokenizes inside one — parameter names,
# filter operands, the trim and notrim keywords, the quoted defaults.
#
# The cost of the gap runs one way only. An author writing a pragma too far down sees it coloured as
# a working directive, and TiddlyWiki renders the backslash line as prose. Nothing colours wrongly in
# the other direction: a pragma in its proper place reads correctly.
#
# BOTH APPROACHES STAND MEASURED. One works, and the cost fell somewhere nobody expected.
#
#   \A anchored region   REFUSED. vscode-textmate feeds the grammar a line at a time, so \A anchors
#                        at each LINE start rather than at the source. \G answers no better: it moves
#                        with the tokenizer, and a region keyed on it opened right after the first
#                        line the injections consumed.
#
#   end-on-first-block   WORKS. Not as a region ending at the first block, but as its inverse: a
#                        nameless region opening at the first line that carries content and opens no
#                        pragma, and never closing. The pragma rules stand OUTSIDE it, so they reach
#                        only what precedes it. Measured: a pragma after prose reads as prose, the
#                        specimen below passes, and `npm run overreach` falls from 161 spans to 104.
#
#                        It costs no nesting level a reader sees — a region carrying no name adds
#                        nothing to a scope stack. It costs two other things, both mendable:
#
#                        A caller reading a field VALUE opens mid-line, where the region's own begin
#                        wants a line start it never sees. Splitting the region's patterns into a
#                        rule of their own and including THAT from the field-value captures answers
#                        it — measured, `multids.lines.tw5.test` goes green.
#
#                        The dialect's sigils stop reading, because a rule at the dialect's top level
#                        cannot reach inside a base region. Injecting them at the base's root scope
#                        answers it — measured, all eight memetic fixtures go green, and the sigil
#                        reads in block contexts the old three-selector list had missed. That fix
#                        stands on its own merit and landed without the region.
#
# WHAT BLOCKS IT: this assertion format. A `.tw5.test` fixture declaring `text.html.tiddlywiki5` gets
# tokenized under the BASE grammar, where its own `# SYNTAX TEST` header reads as a numbered list —
# content, so the pragma zone closes on line 1. Nine fixtures assert pragma colouring below that
# line, and no rearrangement saves them: the header must stand first, and re-scoping a fixture to the
# wrapper grammar drops the base root scope from every span it names.
#
# So closing this gap costs the repository its ability to assert pragma colouring at all, in the
# format it asserts everything else in. That trade wants deciding before the grammar moves.
#
# `npm run overreach` measures the gap; the count falls from 161 to 104 when it closes.

Ordinary prose stands here.

\define greet(name)
#<- - punctuation.definition.directive.tiddlywiki5
hello $name$
\end

\rules except html
#<- - punctuation.definition.directive.tiddlywiki5
